WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
A self-screening jammer rides on the target and fills the radar's receiver with noise.

2
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.203
Whether the radar can still see the target depends on range.

3
00:00:09.853 --> 00:00:15.989
The radar's echo makes a round trip, so its power falls as one over range to the fourth.

4
00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:21.050
The jamming travels only one way, so it falls as one over range squared.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.760
Their ratio, signal to jamming, therefore improves as the target closes.

6
00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:32.579
Burn-through is the range where it reaches what detection needs, 13 decibels here.

7
00:00:32.629 --> 00:00:37.192
Without any processing gain that happens at only about 80 metres.

8
00:00:37.842 --> 00:00:44.145
Pulse compression and Doppler filtering add 20 decibels of gain against noise.

9
00:00:44.195 --> 00:00:48.380
Burn-through moves out tenfold, to about 795 metres.

10
00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:57.208
A stand-off jammer is farther away, and if it sits in the radar's sidelobes its jamming enters through a much lower gain.

11
00:00:57.258 --> 00:01:00.336
Burn-through then reaches about 49 kilometres.

12
00:01:00.386 --> 00:01:05.436
You can explore this in the Studio's RF window, under receiver comparisons.
